From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 19:36:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: qcom: BRINGUP: say when the bark fires, and let it
 be provoked

BRINGUP aid, not for submission.

Every watchdog reset on taimen produces nothing at all: no pretimeout
panic, no hard-lockup report, an empty pstore afterwards, and silence on
two independently verified netconsole transports. The bark path looks
correct on paper -- the DT carries the vendor's SPI 3, qcom,kpss-wdt sets
pretimeout support, set_pretimeout reprograms BARK_TIME, and sysfs reads
back timeout=30 pretimeout=10 governor=panic -- so the open question is
simply whether the interrupt is ever delivered.

That cannot be answered from userspace. sysfs exposes timeout and
pretimeout read-only, systemd holds the chardev open so nothing else can
issue WDIOC_SETPRETIMEOUT, and shortening the bark through systemd's own
RuntimeWatchdogPreSec would take effect from early boot and turn the
phone into a boot loop.

So: log unconditionally from the handler, and add a write-only parameter
that programs a short bark on demand,

  echo 3 > /sys/module/qcom_wdt/parameters/bark_test

leaving the bite 30 s out, which is ample room for the panic to reach
netconsole. If the reset is still silent after this, the bark never
arrived and no CPU was left to service it.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c | 58 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
index 883923ff5946..ad1f9435b153 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
@@ -79,11 +79,68 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_wdt_isr(int irq, void *arg)
 {
 	struct watchdog_device *wdd = arg;
 
+	/*
+	 * BRINGUP: unconditional, and deliberately pr_emerg. The whole reason
+	 * this instrument is in doubt is that a watchdog reset on taimen
+	 * produces NOTHING on any channel -- so the first question is whether
+	 * this handler runs at all. If it does, the panic governor's output
+	 * follows; if the reset is silent even now, the bark never arrived and
+	 * the CPUs were already gone.
+	 */
+	pr_emerg("qcom_wdt: BARK -- pretimeout fired on cpu%d\n",
+		 raw_smp_processor_id());
+
 	watchdog_notify_pretimeout(wdd);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
+/*
+ * BRINGUP: prove the bark end to end without waiting for a real hang.
+ *
+ * The bark cannot be provoked from userspace: sysfs exposes timeout and
+ * pretimeout read-only, and systemd holds the chardev open so no second
+ * opener can issue WDIOC_SETPRETIMEOUT. Shortening the bark through
+ * systemd's own RuntimeWatchdogPreSec would apply from early boot and
+ * boot-loop the phone. So do it here, once, on demand:
+ *
+ *   echo 3 > /sys/module/qcom_wdt/parameters/bark_test
+ *
+ * programs a 3 s bark and pets, leaving the bite 30 s out -- ample room for
+ * the panic to reach netconsole before the secure side resets the SoC.
+ */
+static struct qcom_wdt *qcom_wdt_bark_test_wdt;
+
+static int qcom_wdt_bark_test_set(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
+{
+	struct qcom_wdt *wdt = qcom_wdt_bark_test_wdt;
+	unsigned int secs;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!wdt)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	ret = kstrtouint(val, 0, &secs);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* Must stay well inside the bite, or this proves nothing. */
+	if (!secs || secs >= wdt->wdd.timeout)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	pr_emerg("qcom_wdt: BARK TEST -- arming a %u s bark (bite at %u s)\n",
+		 secs, wdt->wdd.timeout);
+	writel(secs * wdt->rate, wdt_addr(wdt, WDT_BARK_TIME));
+	writel(1, wdt_addr(wdt, WDT_RST));
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct kernel_param_ops qcom_wdt_bark_test_ops = {
+	.set = qcom_wdt_bark_test_set,
+};
+module_param_cb(bark_test, &qcom_wdt_bark_test_ops, NULL, 0200);
+
 static int qcom_wdt_start(struct watchdog_device *wdd)
 {
 	struct qcom_wdt *wdt = to_qcom_wdt(wdd);
@@ -349,6 +406,7 @@ static int qcom_wdt_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, wdt);
+	qcom_wdt_bark_test_wdt = wdt;	/* BRINGUP, see bark_test above */
 	return 0;
 }
 
